<title>Video.length Function</title>


<subheader>Format</subheader>

<logicsyntax>
d = video_name.length();
</logicsyntax>


<subheader>Description</subheader>

The <logiccolor>Video.length</logiccolor> function returns the length, in seconds, of the video held by the <link Video.csdoc>Video</link> object.

The <logiccolor>Video.length</logiccolor> function only supports video recorded in the format that CSPro uses to create recordings: <link "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WebM">WebM</link>. The function will return <logiccolor>default</logiccolor> for non-WebM video files.

Many WebM videos store the length in the file's header, making the calculation quick, but some files, particularly those created from a stream, may not contain that value. In these cases, the length will be calculated by parsing the entire video. This calculation does not account for the length of the video's last <link "https://www.webmproject.org/docs/container/#:~:text=Supported-,Block,-%C2%B6">block</link>, so it may undercount the length by a couple hundreds of a second (e.g., 32.480 vs. 32.478).

If you want to check if a Video object has a valid WebM recording but do not want to potentially parse the entire file to calculate the length, you can determine if a file is a valid WebM by using the <logiccolor>Video.height</logiccolor> or <logiccolor>Video.width</logiccolor> functions.


<subheader>Return Value</subheader>

The function returns the duration in seconds (and fractional seconds) of the video recording or <logiccolor>default</logiccolor> if the duration cannot be computed.


